The received signal at the source
$\Source{2}$, $\y{2}$, is written as


\begin{align} \y{2}
   =
   \h{2} G \y{R} + \h{0} \s{2} + \n{2}
\end{align}


where $G$ is the scalar amplifying gain at the relay, $\h{0}
\sim \CG{0}{\CP{0}}$ is the channel gain for the link between
$\Source{1}$ and $\Source{2}$, $\s{2}= [ {\begin{array}{*{20}c}{-s_2^{\ast} } & {s_1^{\ast} }  \\
\end{array}}]$ is the second row of the Alamouti code, and $\n{2}$ is
the AWGN vector with zero mean and variance $N_0$.

The received signal at the source $\Source{1}$,
$\y{1}$, is shown as


\begin{align} \y{1}
   =
   \h{1} G \y{R} + \h{0} \x{2} + \n{1}
\end{align}


where $\x{2}= [ {\begin{array}{*{20}c}{-x_2^{\ast} } & {x_1^{\ast} }  \\
\end{array}}]$ is the second row of Alamouti code and $\n{1}$ is
the AWGN vector with zero mean and variance $N_0$.

Taking these information into account, we have


\begin{align}
    \EX{\FN{G \y{R}}^2} = \EX{\FN{\s{i}}^2} =\EX{\FN{\x{i}}^2}
\end{align}


yielding


\begin{align} G^2 = \left(|\h{1}|^2+ |\h{2}|^2 + \frac{1}{\bar\gamma}\right)^{-1}
\end{align}


where $\bar\gamma=\mathcal{P}_s/N_0$ is the average signal-to-noise ratio (SNR).

Hence, the received signal at $\Source{1}$ and
$\Source{2}$ given in \eqref{eq:thirdphase} and
\eqref{eq:secondphase} can readily be formed, respectively, as
follows:


\begin{align} \y{1}
    &=
    \B{h} \B{X} + \z{1}
    \\
    \y{2}
    &=
    \B{h} \B{S} + \z{2}
\end{align}


where $\B{h}= [ {\begin{array}{*{20}c}{G\h{1}\h{2} } & {\h{0} }  \\
\end{array}}]$, $\z{1}=G \h{1} \B{n}_{\mathsf{R}} + \n{1}$, $\z{2}=G \h{2} \B{n}_{\mathsf{R}} + \n{2}$, $ \B{S} = \left[ {\begin{array}{*{20}c} {s_1 } & {s_2 }  \\ { - s_2^ *  } & {s_1^ *  }
 \\ \end{array} } \right]$, and $ \B{X} = \left[ {\begin{array}{*{20}c} {x_1 } & {x_2 }  \\ { - x_2^ *  } & {x_1^ *  }
 \\ \end{array} } \right]$. Here, the new noise vector $\z{i}$, with $i \in \{1,2\}$, is the complex Gaussian vector, i.e., $\z{i} \sim \CGV{2}{\B{0}}{N_0\bigl(1+\left|\h{i}\right|^2G^2\bigr)\B{I}_2}$ .

From \eqref{eq:PDF:Z:1},
we have


\begin{align} I_1
    =
    \sum\limits_{u=1}^{2}
    \sum\limits_{v=0}^{1}
    \int\limits_{0}^{\infty}
    \mathcal{A}_v
    \frac{\ln z}{1+z}  z^{u} \exp(-\beta_1 z)
    \Bessel{v}{ \alpha z}
    dz
\end{align}


where


\begin{align*}
    \alpha
    &=
    2\sqrt{\frac{2}{\snravei{1}\snravei{2}}},
    \hspace{0.3cm}
    \beta_1 = \frac{1}{\snravei{2}} + \frac{2}{\snravei{1}}
    \nonumber
    \\
    \mathcal{A}_0 &= \alpha^2,
    \hspace{1.1cm}
    \mathcal{A}_1 = \alpha \beta_1
\end{align*}

Let us rewrite $Z$ in a more tractable form as


\begin{align} Z
    =
    \left(X + Y\right)^{-1}
\end{align}


where $X=1/\snri{i}$ and $Y=2/\snri{j}$.

Then, after some algebraic
simplifications together with the help of
\cite[eq.~(3.471.9)]{GR:00:Book}, the moment generating function
(MGF) of $X$ and $Y$ can be expressed as, respectively


\begin{align} &\MGF{X}{s}
    =
    \EXs{X}{\exp\left(-s x\right)}
    =
    2 \sqrt{\frac{s}{\snravei{i}}} \Bessel{1}{2
    \sqrt{\frac{s}{\snravei{i}}}}
    \\ &\MGF{Y}{s}
    =
    \EXs{Y}{\exp\left(-s y\right)}
    =
    2 \sqrt{\frac{2s}{\snravei{j}}} \Bessel{1}{2
    \sqrt{\frac{2s}{\snravei{j}}}}
\end{align}

As can be seen from \eqref{eq:Z}, since $1/Z$ is the sum of two
statistically independent random variables, by using
\eqref{eq:MGF:X} and \eqref{eq:MGF:Y}, the MGF of $1/Z$ can be
expressed as


\begin{align} \MGF{1/Z}{s}
    =
    4\sqrt{\frac{2}{\snravei{i}\snravei{j}}}s \Bessel{1}{2
    \sqrt{\frac{s}{\snravei{i}}}}
    \Bessel{1}{2
    \sqrt{\frac{2s}{\snravei{j}}}}
\end{align}

Then, the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of $1/Z$,
$\CDF{1/Z}{x}$, can be shown as


\begin{eqnarray} \CDF{1/Z}{x}
     =1- \mathcal{L}^{-1}\{\MGF{1/Z}{s}/s\}|_{1/x}
\end{eqnarray}


where $\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{.\}$ stands for the inverse Laplace
transform.

From \eqref{eq:MGF:1:Z} and \eqref{eq:CDF:1:Z} together
with \cite[eq.~(13.2.20)]{RK:66:Book}, we have



\begin{align} \CDF{Z}{z}
    =
    1 - \alpha z \exp\left(-\beta_i z\right)
    \Bessel{1}{\alpha z}
\end{align}


where $\alpha = 2\sqrt{\frac{2}{\snravei{i}\snravei{j}}}$ and
$\beta_i = \frac{1}{\snravei{j}} + \frac{2}{\snravei{i}}$.

